ria that expanded into northern India in the first centuries CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/kushano-sasanian-kingdom"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/kushano-sasanian-kingdom.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Kushano-Sasanian Kingdom</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Kushano-Sasanian Kingdom was the realm of Sasanian princes who governed Bactria and parts of Gandhara after the Sasanian conquest of the western Kushan lands in the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/kushinagar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/kushinagar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Kushinagar</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Kushinagar, ancient Kusinara, is a town in eastern Uttar Pradesh, India, where according to Buddhist tradition the Buddha died and entered parinirvana.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/kushite-empire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Kushite Empire</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Kushite Empire, or Kingdom of Kush, was a Nubian state on the middle Nile in present-day Sudan, centred successively on Kerma, Napata and Meroe.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/kusugu-well"><span class="enc-name">Kusugu Well</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Kusugu well is a historic well in Daura, in Katsina State, northern Nigeria, at the centre of the Bayajidda legend.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/kusunoki-masashige"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/kusunoki-masashige.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Kusunoki Masashige</span><span